---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I unit-test app code that calls Traverse without running WASM?"
  description="Use the deterministic test double that ships in each embedder package. Script outputs and errors per target, assert on stable event JSON, and keep real WASM for integration tests."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-unit-test-app-code-that-calls-traverse.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>Swap the real embedder for the test double that ships in the same package. Your screen, handler, or job talks to the embedder API: submit, subscribe, shutdown. The double implements that same API without loading a bundle or any WASM. You tell it what each target should return, and your test checks how your code reacts.</p>

  <h2>JS/TS and Rust: <code>EmbedderTestDouble</code></h2>
  <p>Both <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code> and the Rust <code>traverse-embedder@0.15.0</code> crate export <code>EmbedderTestDouble</code>. For each target id you can script a success with an output, or a failure with a runtime-shaped error code and message.</p>

  <pre><code>{tsExample}</code></pre>

  <pre><code>{rustExample}</code></pre>

  <p>Why it's useful for assertions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Same event envelope as production.</strong> Events carry <code>kind: "embedder_event"</code>, <code>event_id</code>, <code>sequence</code>, <code>event_type</code>, and <code>data</code>, just like a real <code>BundleEmbedder</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Deterministic ids.</strong> Sessions, requests, and events are numbered (<code>sess-00000001</code>, <code>req-00000001</code>, <code>evt-00000001</code>), so snapshot tests don't flake.</li>
    <li><strong>Real boundary failures.</strong> An unknown target is rejected with <code>target_not_found</code>. Anything after <code>shutdown()</code> is rejected with <code>runtime_stopped</code>. Shutdown is idempotent.</li>
    <li><strong>Trace browsing works too.</strong> <code>traceList</code> and <code>traceGet</code> return the same safe trace records the real embedder does, so a trace viewer can be tested without WASM.</li>
    <li><strong>Compatible-capability lifecycle.</strong> <code>withCompatibleTarget</code> lets you test start, stop, and kill, including the <code>platforms[]</code> allowlist guard.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Swift, Kotlin, and .NET: <code>InMemoryTraverseEmbedder</code></h2>
  <p>The native packages ship a smaller deterministic harness called <code>InMemoryTraverseEmbedder</code>. You set one target output with <code>withTargetOutput</code>, submit, and read ordered runtime-shaped events back. It also covers compatible start, stop, and kill. It doesn't have per-target scripting or trace browsing. These packages are published but pre-1.0, and none is Certified yet.</p>

  <h2>What a test double doesn't test</h2>
  <p>It never runs the capability. A double doesn't validate the contract, check the bundle digest, or load <code>runtime.wasm</code>, so it can't tell you the pricing rule is right. Test that in two other places:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The capability itself</strong>, against its contract, before it ships. See <a href="/questions/how-do-i-test-a-capability-before-shipping-it-to-multiple-platforms.html">how to test a capability before shipping it to multiple platforms</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>One real integration test</strong> per host that loads your actual bundle with <code>BundleEmbedder</code> and runs discover, execute, and trace end to end. The web package's own suite does this with the checked-in <code>traverse-starter</code> bundle.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>In <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.14.0</code>, <code>releaseEvidence().package.version</code> reported <code>0.9.0</code> because the version was hard-coded (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1654" target="_blank" rel="noopener">#1654</a>). The published 0.15.0 package sets that constant to <code>0.15.0</code>.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
